"EGRES technical report TR-2024-15 (https://egres.elte.hu/www/tr-24-15.html)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; #### TR-2024-15 (arXiv:2412.15457) ... In this paper, we study a special case of this setting, the Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture, which states that any graph on n vertices formed by the union of n-1 spanning arborescences contains an arborescence using exactly one arc from each.\n\n#### TR-2024-15 (arXiv:2412.15457)\n\n## Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture\n\n#### Kristóf Bérczi, Tamás Király, Yutaro Yamaguchi, Yu Yokoi\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe famous Ryser--Brualdi--Stein conjecture asserts that every n×n Latin square contains a partial transversal of size n-1. Since its appearance, the conjecture has attracted significant interest, leading to several generalizations. One of the most notable extensions is to matroid intersection given by Aharoni, Kotlar, and Ziv, focusing on the existence of a common independent transversal of the common independent sets of two matroids. In this paper, we study a special case of this setting, the Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture, which states that any graph on n vertices formed by the union of n-1 spanning arborescences contains an arborescence using exactly one arc from each. We prove that the computational problem of testing the existence of such an arborescence with a fixed root is NP-complete, verify the conjecture in several cases, and explore relaxed versions of the problem.\n\nBibtex entry:\n\n@techreport{egres-24-15,\nAUTHOR  | =  | {B{\\'e}rczi, Krist{\\'o}f and Kir{\\'a}ly, Tam{\\'a}s and Yamaguchi, Yutaro and Yokoi, Yu},\nTITLE  | =  | {Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture},\nNOTE  | =  | {{\\tt egres.elte.hu}},\nINSTITUTION  | =  | {Egerv{\\'a}ry Research Group, Budapest},\nYEAR  | =  | {2024},\nNUMBER  | =  | {TR-2024-15}\n}--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture. (https://dblp.uni-trier.de/rec/journals/corr/abs-2412-15457.html)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: today; # \"Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture.\" ... DOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2412.15457\n\n# \"Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture.\"\n\nKristóf Bérczi et al. (2024)\n\nDOI: 10.48550/ARXIV.2412.15457\n\naccess: openImage\n\ntype: Informal or Other Publication\n\nmetadata version: 2025-12-07\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2412-15457\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2412-15457\n\nKristóf BércziImage: 0000-0003-0457-4573, Tamás Király, Yutaro Yamaguchi, Yu Yokoi:\nRainbow Arborescence Conjecture. CoRR abs/2412.15457 (2024)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublications (https://tkiraly.web.elte.hu/pub/)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Yokoi, Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture, arXiv:2412.15457 (2024)\n## Publications of Tamás Király\n\n \n\n### Published and submitted papers\n\nK. Bérczi, T. Király, Y. Yamaguchi, Y. Yokoi, The Rainbow Arborescence Problem on Cycles, arXiv:2511.04953 (2025)\n\nK. Bérczi, K. Chandrasekaran, T. Király, D.P. Szabo, {s,t}-Separating Principal Partition Sequence of Submodular Functions, IPCO 2026, arXiv:2510.25664 (2025)\n\nK. Bérczi, K. Chandrasekaran, T. Király, D.P. Szabo, Approximating Submodular Matroid-Constrained Partitioning, Theoretical Computer Science, Volume 1073 (2026)\n\nK. Bérczi, T. Király, Y. Yamaguchi, Y. Yokoi, Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture, arXiv:2412.15457 (2024)\n\nG. Csáji, T. Király, Y. Yokoi, A simple 1.5-approximation algorithm for a wide range of maximum size stable matching problems, Mathematics of Operations Research, available online (2025)\n\nT. Fleiner, A. Frank, T. Király, A new approach to bipartite stable matching optimization, arXiv:2409.04885 (2024)\n\nG. Csáji, T. Király, Zs. Mészáros-Karkus, The NTU Partitioned Matching Game for International Kidney Exchange Programs, Mathematics of Operations Research, available online (2026), arXiv:2409.01452 (2024)\n\nM. Bárász, K. Bérczi, T. Király, Y. Yamaguchi, Y. Yokoi, Matroid Intersection under Minimum Rank Oracle, arXiv:2407.03229 (2024)\n\nK. Bérczi, T. Király, Y. Kobayashi, Y. Yamaguchi, Y. Yokoi, Finding Spanning Trees with Perfect Matchings, Discrete Applied Mathematics 371 (2025), 137-147, arXiv:2407.02958 (2024)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2412.15457] Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture (https://toehanger.shop/?_=%2Fabs%2F2412.15457%23KGzYqmHwYljVikqzZcqJdSc%3D)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; This motivated the formulation of the Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture: any graph on $n$ vertices formed by the union of $n-1$ spanning arborescences contains an arborescence using exactly one arc from each. ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2412.15457 [math.CO]\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 19 Dec 2024 (v1), last revised 9 Dec 2025 (this version, v2)]\n\n# Title:Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture\n\nAuthors:Kristóf Bérczi, Tamás Király, Yutaro Yamaguchi, Yu Yokoi\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture, by Krist\\'of B\\'erczi and Tam\\'as Kir\\'aly and Yutaro Yamaguchi and Yu Yokoi\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:The famous Ryser--Brualdi--Stein conjecture asserts that every $k \\times k$ Latin square contains a partial transversal of size $k-1$. Since its appearance, the conjecture has attracted significant interest, leading to several proposed generalizations. One of the most notable of these, by Aharoni, Kotlar, and Ziv, conjectures that $k$ disjoint common bases of two matroids of rank $k$ have a common independent partial transversal of size $k-1$. Although simple counterexamples show that the size $k-1$ above cannot be improved to $k$ (i.e., a transversal instead of a partial transversal), it is remarkable that no such counterexample is known for the special case of spanning arborescences. This motivated the formulation of the Rainbow Arborescence Conjecture: any graph on $n$ vertices formed by the union of $n-1$ spanning arborescences contains an arborescence using exactly one arc from each.\n> We prove several partial results on this conjecture. We show that the computational problem of testing the existence of such an arborescence with a fixed root is NP-complete, verify the conjecture in several special cases, and study relaxations of the problem. In particular, we establish the validity of the conjecture when the underlying undirected graph is a cycle; this also yields a new result on systems of distinct representatives for intervals on a cycle.\n\nComments:  | 27 pages, 31 figures; integrating arXiv:2511.04953\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM); Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2412.15457 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2412.15457v2 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.15457 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Yutaro Yamaguchi [view email]\n[v1] Thu, 19 Dec 2024 23:31:03 UTC (13 KB)\n[v2] Tue, 9 Dec 2025 09:18:54 UTC (900 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThis electronic thesis or dissertation has been (https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/files/458020560/Final_Copy_2025_05_13_Huang_H_PhD_Redacted.pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; of permutation groups, submitted (2024), arXiv:2410.22613. ... the generalised Saxl graph Σ(G) is G-arc-transitive in this setting (see Theorem 5.3 below), ... establish Conjecture II for some base-two diagonal type groups in Theorem 5.6.\nC H A P T E R\n5\nDIAGONAL TYPE GROUPS\nThe majority of the new results in this chapter are taken from the papers\nS.D. Freedman, H.Y. Huang, M. Lee and K. Rekvényi, On the generalised Saxl graphs\nof permutation groups, submitted (2024), arXiv:2410.22613.\nH.Y. Huang, Base sizes of primitive groups of diagonal type, Forum Math. Sigma 12\n(2024), Paper No. e2, 43 pp.\nwhich are [52] and [71], respectively. The work in Sections 5.8 and 5.9 is my own, original and\nunpublished work.\nIn this chapter, we will consider the diagonal type primitive groups. Our main goal is to\nestablish Theorem A, which determines the exact base sizes of all these groups. A precise\nstatement of this result is Theorem 5.1 below. We will also classify the groups G such that\nthe generalised Saxl graph Σ(G) is G-arc-transitive in this setting (see Theorem 5.3 below),\nestablishing Theorem D. Additionally, we will make progress towards a classification of the\nsemi-Frobenius primitive groups of diagonal type, which is Theorem 5.4. Finally, we will also\nestablish Conjecture II for some base-two diagonal type groups in Theorem 5.6.\nWith the exception of Theorem 5.6 and the results in Sections 5.8 and 5.9, the majority of\nthe new results in this chapter are taken from my single-authored paper [71], and the results\nconcerning the generalised Saxl graphs come from my joint paper [52] with Freedman, Lee and\nRekvényi.\n91--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBounds in radial Moore graphs of diameter 3 | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2411.19587)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; In this paper we study upper bounds for both the maximum number of central vertices and the status of radial Moore graphs. ... Ceresuela and Nacho López}, year={2024}, eprint={2411.19587}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.19587}, }\nSubmitted 29 Nov 2024\n\n# Bounds in radial Moore graphs of diameter 3\n\nUniversitat de Lleida\n\nJM\n\nJesús M. Ceresuela\n\nNL\n\nNacho López\n\n## Abstract\n\nRadial Moore graphs are approximations of Moore graphs that preserve the distance-preserving spanning tree for its central vertices. One way to classify their resemblance with a Moore graph is the status measure. The status of a graph is defined as the sum of the distances of all pairs of ordered vertices and equals twice the Wiener index. In this paper we study upper bounds for both the maximum number of central vertices and the status of radial Moore graphs. Finally, we present a family of radial Moore graphs of diameter $3$3 that is conjectured to have maximum status.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{ceresuela2024boundsradialmooregraphs, title={Bounds in radial Moore graphs of diameter 3}, author={Jesús M. Ceresuela and Nacho López}, year={2024}, eprint={2411.19587}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.19587}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHongyi Huang (https://hongyihuang328.github.io/research.html)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; My main interest of research is in group theory, mostly finite groups. ... On valency problems of Saxl graphs ... arXiv:2410.22613 | doi:10.5802/alco.493 | MR5094497\nJournal of Algebraic Combinatorics 61 (2025), Paper No. 57, 44 pp.\narXiv:2404.18753 | doi:10.1007/s10801-025-01425-3 | MR4925294\n  7. On the intersections of Sylow subgroups in almost simple groups\njoint with T.C. Burness\nJournal of Algebra 690 (2026), 596-631.\narXiv:2506.19745 | doi:10.1016/j.jalgebra.2025.11.003 | MR4990925\n  8. On the intersections of nilpotent subgroups of simple groups\njoint with T.C. Burness\nProceedings of the London Mathematical Society 132 (2026), Paper No. e70134, 48 pp.\narXiv:2508.03479 | doi:10.1112/plms.70134 | MR5048571\n  9. On the generalised Saxl graphs of permutation groups\njoint with S.D. Freedman, M. Lee and K. Rekvényi\nAlgebraic Combinatorics 9 (2026), 611-648.\narXiv:2410.22613 | doi:10.5802/alco.493 | MR5094497\n  10. Greedy bases and relational complexity of diagonal type groups\njoint wit